raised from its 140-year-old watery grave in 2002,

20
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:02,940
reveal a quantum leap in technology beyond any previous conflict.

21
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:07,120
Today's modern warship was born right here.

22
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:12,220
This is the first instance of the movable turret

23
00:02:12,221 --> 00:02:16,580
that allowed every gun to be brought to bear on any target.

24
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:23,380
This was the death knell of the old system.

25
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:26,420
The armored protection for guns was born here.

26
00:02:26,960 --> 00:02:30,280
This is the granddaddy of them all, the USS Monitor.

27
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:36,180
The technology of the Civil War reaches back to the Industrial Revolution

28
00:02:36,181 --> 00:02:39,500
that began in the last decades of the 18th century in England.

29
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:43,720
It was a time of profound transformation

30
00:02:43,721 --> 00:02:46,520
that resulted in new manufacturing processes.

31
00:02:46,521 --> 00:02:51,740
By the 1850s, the Industrial Revolution was firmly established in America.

32
00:02:51,741 --> 00:02:55,740
I think the key components in the Industrial Revolution is the idea of using machinery

33
00:02:55,741 --> 00:02:57,660
to do what used to be done by hand.

34
00:02:57,661 --> 00:03:01,020
And also, with machine-made production, you have interchangeability.

35
00:03:01,021 --> 00:03:04,820
With interchangeability, you can mass produce things.

36
00:03:04,821 --> 00:03:09,760
By the mid-19th century, mass production industries had been developed mainly in the North, which

37
00:03:09,761 --> 00:03:13,760
controlled 80% of U.S. factories.

38
00:03:13,761 --> 00:03:18,600
But in 1860, 84% of all Southerners worked in agriculture.

39
00:03:18,601 --> 00:03:21,440
The cotton industry was booming in the South.

40
00:03:21,441 --> 00:03:24,440
The differences between these two separate societies,

41
00:03:24,441 --> 00:03:26,540
coupled with the issue of slavery,

42
00:03:26,541 --> 00:03:28,040
set the stage for the conflict.

43
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:33,580
Industrial technologies were transformed

44
00:03:33,581 --> 00:03:35,540
from peacetime to war.

45
00:03:35,541 --> 00:03:39,020
The Industrial Revolution harnessed man's intellect

46
00:03:39,021 --> 00:03:41,680
with interchangeable parts to create

47
00:03:41,681 --> 00:03:45,360
some of the most devastating killing devices known to man in that era.

48
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:55,260
At the beginning of the Civil War,

49
00:03:55,740 --> 00:03:59,240
infantry soldiers were armed with the smoothbore muzzle-loading musket

50
00:03:59,241 --> 00:04:01,800
in use since the 1600s.

51
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:06,540
The weapon was time-consuming to load,

52
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:09,420
and its rate of fire was three rounds per minute

53
00:04:09,421 --> 00:04:12,660
with an accurate range of under 100 yards.

54
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:18,000
This is a smoothbore muzzle-loading musket.

55
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:20,920
Paper cartridge containing the bullet and powder

56
00:04:20,921 --> 00:04:22,660
will be withdrawn from the cartridge box.

57
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:25,160
The cartridge will then be formed with the teeth.

58
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:27,220
The powder charge will be dumped down the barrel,

59
00:04:27,620 --> 00:04:29,100
and the ramrod will be withdrawn.

60
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:33,080
You'd use the ramrod to ram the cartridge down the musket barrel.

61
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:37,540
Then the musket could be cocked, take a priming cap,

62
00:04:37,541 --> 00:04:40,240
place it on the nipple, and the musket will be ready to fire.

63
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:46,320
The American muzzleloader was grossly inaccurate

64
00:04:46,321 --> 00:04:50,980
because a ball did not fit tightly in the muzzle or the barrel of a gun.

65
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:58,160
Once the gun was discharged, the ball literally did not cling to the barrel.

66
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:02,780
It bounced around from left to right or up or down as it left the barrel.

67
00:05:02,781 --> 00:05:03,660
Hey! Hey!

68
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:05,900
Fuck!

69
00:05:07,540 --> 00:05:13,860
Two new technological innovations dramatically improved the accuracy and range of the weapons used during the Civil War.

70
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:17,320
The first was rifling.

71
00:05:18,080 --> 00:05:21,980
The process involved cutting spiral grooves into the inside of the gun barrel.

72
00:05:22,860 --> 00:05:27,560
This gave the projectile a spiral twist as it was fired, increasing the accuracy.

73
00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:33,000
Rifling, practiced since the 1600s, was done by hand.

74
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,920
However, by the time of the Civil War,

75
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:39,540
rifling a barrel with a machine lathe was far more precise,

76
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:41,660
and weapons could be mass-produced.

77
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:44,340
This machine operated and had a cutting edge in it.

78
00:05:45,780 --> 00:05:48,240
And as the barrel was turned around the cutting edge,

79
00:05:48,320 --> 00:05:50,600
it would groove into the barrel and it would have rifling.

80
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:57,440
Then, the round musket ball, in use since the 1400s,

81
00:05:57,441 --> 00:06:00,060
was redesigned into a cone-shaped slug

82
00:06:00,061 --> 00:06:02,540
about half an inch in diameter and an inch long.

83
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,980
It is largely attributed to a Frenchman, Claude Minet.

84
00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:10,860
He invented or conceived of a ballistic-shaped missile.

85
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:14,700
At first, it was called the Minet Ball,

86
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:17,420
but Americans simply referred to it as the mini ball.

87
00:06:19,700 --> 00:06:22,860
When fired, the gases expelled from the powder

88
00:06:22,861 --> 00:06:26,100
expanded the mini ball into the rifling grooves of the gun barrel,

89
00:06:26,820 --> 00:06:29,820
causing the projectile to spiral at an accelerated velocity,

90
00:06:29,821 --> 00:06:34,020
making it go farther, faster, and straighter.

91
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:37,700
The mini ball gave us the capability to fight at long range

92
00:06:37,701 --> 00:06:42,860
and to engage troops at a distance of 2, 3, or up to 400 yards.

93
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:44,280
It was long-range fighting.

94
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:51,480
From 1861 and throughout the war,

95
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:55,660
over a million and a half Springfield rifled muskets were mass produced,

96
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:58,780
most of which were purchased by the Union Army.

97
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:06,240
The Springfield's rate of fire was still three rounds a minute.

98
00:07:07,460 --> 00:07:11,160
However, its range was vastly improved to 500 yards.

99
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:13,540
This is an 1861 Springfield muzzleloader.

100
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:18,360
Since the Union invested in the Springfield early in the war,

101
00:07:18,740 --> 00:07:21,360
it became the basic infantry weapon used in the conflict.

102
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:27,760
However, weapons technology continued to improve

103
00:07:27,761 --> 00:07:31,760
that loaded from the breech, or back end of the barrel.

104
00:07:31,840 --> 00:07:35,760
A popular breech-loader model was the Sharps carbine rifle,

105
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:39,840
used by the Union cavalry in the first three years of the war.

106
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,920
The Sharps rifle rate of fire was up to 10 rounds a minute,

107
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:48,640
with a range of 1,200 yards.

108
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:54,760
In order to load the rifle, the shooter would drop the breech,

109
00:07:54,761 --> 00:07:58,760
drop the breech, insert the cartridge with the bullet and powder,

110
00:07:58,761 --> 00:08:01,760
insert it into the chamber, raising the breech block,

111
00:08:01,761 --> 00:08:05,760
trimming the back of the cartridge off, exposing the powder to the primer.

112
00:08:05,761 --> 00:08:08,760
The nipple will then be primed with the percussion cap,

113
00:08:08,761 --> 00:08:12,760
the hammer will be cocked, and the rifle will be fired.

114
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:16,760
And this again would be an incredible innovation at that time

115
00:08:16,761 --> 00:08:19,760
when you could only get in one or two shots

116
00:08:19,761 --> 00:08:22,760
from the old single-loaded muzzleloaders of that time.

117
00:08:22,761 --> 00:08:26,980
So these new breech loaders will be a very definite link across the ages

118
00:08:26,981 --> 00:08:29,460
between the Civil War and modern-day weaponry.

119
00:08:34,940 --> 00:08:40,640
Fire power continued to improve when B. Tyler Henry invented a rapid fire rifle.

120
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:49,520
From 1862 to 1866, 13,000 Henry Repeater rifles were manufactured.

121
00:08:49,521 --> 00:08:50,221
appeared.

122
00:08:50,222 --> 00:08:55,400
Its rate of fire was up to 30 rounds per minute with a range of 1,000 yards.

123
00:08:55,401 --> 00:09:03,920
This is the Henry. It is a repeater. It's a 13 shot. To load it, slide the magazine

124
00:09:03,921 --> 00:09:08,700
follower all the way forward, unlock the magazine tube.

125
00:09:08,701 --> 00:09:12,900
Its spring-loaded magazine used a fully self-contained rimfire metallic cartridge.

126
00:09:12,901 --> 00:09:18,340
You close it, hold your follower, follow your ammunition down, lever the action, which will

127
00:09:18,341 --> 00:09:25,340
bring a shell up. Already got another one in the chamber.

128
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:32,440
The soldiers were so enamored with the Henry rifle that they finally said you could load

129
00:09:32,441 --> 00:09:37,280
it on Sunday and fire it all week.

130
00:09:37,281 --> 00:09:42,780
Commanders never realized the effectiveness, the deadliness of these weapons in plotting

131
00:09:42,781 --> 00:09:46,420
strategy.

132
00:09:46,421 --> 00:09:52,180
They still resort to the old mass and maneuver traditions of the 19th century, sending waves

133
00:09:52,181 --> 00:09:54,860
of men forward literally to their deaths.

134
00:09:54,861 --> 00:09:59,620
You see this no more tragically than in Pickett's Charge at the Battle of Gettysburg, where

135
00:09:59,621 --> 00:10:02,580
15,000 men started across that field.

136
00:10:02,581 --> 00:10:07,100
Half of them were killed before they got to the Union lines.

137
00:10:07,101 --> 00:10:11,180
Fire!

138
00:10:11,181 --> 00:10:15,940
The same technological advancements that accompanied the rifle were also introduced

139
00:10:15,941 --> 00:10:17,700
to the cannon.

140
00:10:17,701 --> 00:10:19,440
Before the war, the typical cannon

141
00:10:19,441 --> 00:10:22,480
was a muzzle-loading smooth-bore weapon

142
00:10:22,481 --> 00:10:24,880
with a rate of fire of three rounds a minute

143
00:10:24,881 --> 00:10:28,080
and a range of 2,000 yards.

144
00:10:28,081 --> 00:10:29,880
Fire!

145
00:10:29,881 --> 00:10:32,780
This cannon is a muzzle-loading cannon.

146
00:10:32,781 --> 00:10:36,560
It loads the ammunition down the barrel,

147
00:10:36,561 --> 00:10:39,220
and the men on the front of the gun

148
00:10:39,221 --> 00:10:40,700
do all the cleaning of the barrel,

149
00:10:40,701 --> 00:10:43,300
and they also do the loading of the new round.

150
00:10:43,301 --> 00:10:45,940
The men at the rear of the gun prime the piece,

151
00:10:45,941 --> 00:10:46,980
and fire the piece.

152
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:47,980
Fire!

153
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:54,420
This cannon was very effective at close range,

154
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:57,680
but had an effective range of only one mile.

155
00:10:58,040 --> 00:11:00,780
It could lob a shell up to a mile and a half,

156
00:11:00,940 --> 00:11:03,060
but it could not place it on target properly.

157
00:11:09,720 --> 00:11:11,200
Robert Parker Parrott,

158
00:11:11,780 --> 00:11:13,560
a retired Union artillery captain

159
00:11:13,561 --> 00:11:15,760
who turned to metal foundry work as a civilian,

160
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:19,560
made advances in the technology of manufacturing cast iron,

161
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:23,780
which allowed industry to create stronger cannon barrels that could be rifled.

162
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:27,800
The pilot is deadly at 3,200 yards, almost two miles.

163
00:11:28,280 --> 00:11:31,660
So artillery efficiency, artillery deadliness almost doubles

164
00:11:31,661 --> 00:11:34,980
thanks to simply the concept of rifling a cannon barrel.

165
00:11:38,680 --> 00:11:42,800
Toward the end of the war, long-range weapons such as the rifled-barreled

166
00:11:42,801 --> 00:11:45,180
breech-loading cannon were imported from Europe.

167
00:11:45,940 --> 00:11:49,500
Its rate of fire was up to six rounds a minute with a range of two miles.

168
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:54,300
The breach loading system has a wedge in the back of the gun,

169
00:11:54,420 --> 00:11:56,620
which allows you to open it and expose the chamber.

170
00:11:56,621 --> 00:12:01,280
At that point, you place a projectile in and your charge bag.

171
00:12:02,220 --> 00:12:03,580
It's tamped home.

172
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:08,880
You're allowed to slide the breach closed and lock the breach.

173
00:12:09,480 --> 00:12:11,080
And the piece is ready to fire.

174
00:12:12,340 --> 00:12:13,040
Fire!

175
00:12:13,041 --> 00:12:21,440
It is from the breech-loading and the rifling of Civil War cannon that today's modern artillery come.

176
00:12:21,780 --> 00:12:23,780
The connection is direct and unbroken.

177
00:12:29,340 --> 00:12:32,200
Civil War battles were not only fought on land.

178
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:37,080
Sea power played a pivotal role in the war between the states.

179
00:12:37,081 --> 00:12:44,080
The navies on both sides searched for ways to control ports and the high seas.

180
00:12:44,081 --> 00:12:52,080
The tactical solution would come from shipbuilding technology that would change the course of maritime history.

181
00:12:52,081 --> 00:13:02,080
During the Civil War, the price of a Springfield rifle was $15 to $20, which was about a month's pay for a union private.

182
00:13:02,081 --> 00:13:05,080
Civil War Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

183
00:13:05,081 --> 00:13:05,980
marvels.

184
00:13:12,280 --> 00:13:15,800
Today's United States Navy is the most powerful armada the world

185
00:13:15,801 --> 00:13:16,501
has ever known.

186
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:23,460
From mighty warships to undetectable nuclear

187
00:13:23,461 --> 00:13:35,080
submarines, it has an uncompromised global reach.

188
00:13:35,081 --> 00:13:42,080
All these modern naval warships have one thing in common, a

189
00:13:42,081 --> 00:13:49,080
frame and hull of metal. The effective use of metal was

190
00:13:49,081 --> 00:13:55,080
first realized with ironclad ships during the Civil War.

191
00:13:55,081 --> 00:14:00,080
Civil War ironclads sort of made the molds from which every

192
00:14:00,081 --> 00:14:05,780
sort of made the molds from which every military vessel from then on was cast.

193
00:14:07,880 --> 00:14:10,580
Deploying conventional wooden warships of the time,

194
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:15,940
the primary strategy of the Union was to create a complete naval blockade of the southern states.

195
00:14:16,580 --> 00:14:18,920
It was called the Anaconda Plan.

196
00:14:19,480 --> 00:14:24,000
Like the beast that it's named after, the Anaconda, of course, strangles its prey.

197
00:14:24,001 --> 00:14:31,380
And that was the idea that the Union had, was to strangle the Confederacy by blockading her ports.

198
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:39,600
The Confederacy's answer to the Anaconda Plan was one word, ironclads.

199
00:14:41,140 --> 00:14:46,900
In 1861, the Confederates raised the Merrimack, a sunken Union ship,

200
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:51,380
and attached armor plating, creating one of the first ironclad ships.

201
00:14:51,381 --> 00:14:56,180
The vessel was renamed the CSS Virginia.

202
00:14:58,020 --> 00:15:02,760
It was an enormous warship, 262 feet in length,

203
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:06,840
with a beam of 51 feet, weighing 3,200 tons.

204
00:15:07,860 --> 00:15:10,240
It was protected by four inches of iron plating,

205
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:12,580
and with a top speed of eight knots,

206
00:15:13,260 --> 00:15:16,820
it took the Virginia half an hour to make a 180-degree turn.

207
00:15:16,821 --> 00:15:22,120
And so the Virginia becomes kind of a hybrid ship.

208
00:15:22,300 --> 00:15:30,960
She's not a sailing vessel, but she's not going to be too far away from the technology that came before her,

209
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:34,600
in that she was still going to have to fight broadside.

210
00:15:36,040 --> 00:15:39,040
While the South was busy turning the Merrimack into the Virginia,

211
00:15:39,840 --> 00:15:42,820
the North found out about the top secret project through spies.

212
00:15:44,280 --> 00:15:46,740
So the Union built its own secret weapon.

213
00:15:46,820 --> 00:15:48,680
the ironclad monitor.

214
00:15:50,060 --> 00:15:51,700
John Erickson, a Swedish designer,

215
00:15:52,220 --> 00:15:54,760
conceived and designed the novel steam-powered warship.

216
00:15:55,420 --> 00:15:57,040
It was an incredibly brilliant design.

217
00:15:57,580 --> 00:16:01,000
It broke the bonds of the conventional ship design.

218
00:16:02,780 --> 00:16:05,060
The monitor was smaller than the Virginia

219
00:16:05,061 --> 00:16:09,160
with a length of 173 feet with a beam of 41 feet,

220
00:16:09,800 --> 00:16:11,760
weighing 987 tons.

221
00:16:13,720 --> 00:16:16,020
Its top speed was 8 knots.

222
00:16:16,820 --> 00:16:19,740
Eight inches of armor protected the innovative gun turret,

223
00:16:20,260 --> 00:16:24,540
which used two steam-powered engines to revolve 360 degrees.

224
00:16:25,320 --> 00:16:30,580
In that it allowed a relatively small vessel with a relatively few number of guns,

225
00:16:30,660 --> 00:16:31,480
in this case two.

226
00:16:32,340 --> 00:16:37,400
It could bring both guns to bear at the target at any angle to the vessel.

227
00:16:38,400 --> 00:16:41,400
In the first confrontation between two ironclads,

228
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:44,840
the Monitor met the Virginia at Hampton Roads Harbor,

229
00:16:44,841 --> 00:16:47,860
off the state of Virginia in 1862.

230
00:16:49,620 --> 00:16:52,160
The Confederate vessel was already at battle,

231
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:55,460
having crippled or sunk several Union wooden naval vessels

232
00:16:55,461 --> 00:16:57,520
when the Monitor entered the scene.

233
00:16:58,140 --> 00:17:00,060
When the Virginia encounters the Monitor,

234
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:02,000
the Virginia seems to have the advantage.

235
00:17:02,100 --> 00:17:04,120
She has 10 guns to the Monitor's, too.

236
00:17:04,780 --> 00:17:08,720
However, the Monitor has something that no other ship in the world at that time had,

237
00:17:08,721 --> 00:17:10,340
a revolving gun turret.

238
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:13,900
The Monitor was hit 22 times,

239
00:17:13,901 --> 00:17:16,180
and the Virginia 20 by cannon fire

240
00:17:16,181 --> 00:17:17,800
during their four-hour battle.

241
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:23,880
However, neither vessel achieved a clear victory

242
00:17:23,881 --> 00:17:26,100
since all the shells bounced off the arm.

243
00:17:28,600 --> 00:17:30,920
In the end, the battle was a draw,

244
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:33,500
with each side believing it had won.

245
00:17:34,820 --> 00:17:37,060
The two ironclads never fought again.

246
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:39,400
However, the real victory went

247
00:17:39,401 --> 00:17:42,080
to a future of iron and steel warships.

248
00:17:42,081 --> 00:17:45,760
A new technology had overcome naval weapons,

249
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:50,800
and thereafter, the war befall any nation that continued to build wooden frigates

250
00:17:50,801 --> 00:17:52,980
because the ironclads were now here.

251
00:17:55,600 --> 00:17:57,340
Although never sunk in battle,

252
00:17:57,860 --> 00:18:01,760
the Monitor was lost during a storm on New Year's Eve in 1862.

253
00:18:04,340 --> 00:18:07,420
More than 100 years later, in 1973,

254
00:18:07,900 --> 00:18:11,520
the Monitor was discovered in 240 feet of water,

255
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:15,340
16 miles off the coast of Cape Hatteras, North Carolina.

256
00:18:17,540 --> 00:18:21,080
Through painstaking effort, parts of the vessel have been raised,

257
00:18:21,460 --> 00:18:23,100
including the famous revolving turret.

258
00:18:26,120 --> 00:18:30,340
A prized artifact was taken to the Mariner's Museum in Newport News, Virginia,

259
00:18:30,720 --> 00:18:32,060
where it rests today.

260
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:37,820
It's housed in an enormous tank,

261
00:18:38,680 --> 00:18:41,580
containing a solution which will gradually remove the salt

262
00:18:41,581 --> 00:18:45,520
that has accumulated during almost a century and a half on the ocean floor.

263
00:18:51,940 --> 00:18:53,760
However, on this rare occasion,

264
00:18:54,460 --> 00:18:57,820
the Mariner's Museum has agreed to empty the tank for modern marvels

265
00:18:57,821 --> 00:19:02,600
so that we may take our cameras inside this revolutionary historic monument.

266
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:08,560
The process takes hours,

267
00:19:08,561 --> 00:19:12,300
but eventually we set eyes on a true technological wonder.

268
00:19:16,380 --> 00:19:21,120
When the Monitor sank, it turned and rested on the bottom upside down.

269
00:19:23,460 --> 00:19:27,900
So to preserve its fragile state, the turret remains inverted in the tank.

270
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:33,120
Monitor's turret is a truly unique place.

271
00:19:33,121 --> 00:19:40,120
We are one of the few people to be inside of it since it sank in 1862.

272
00:19:40,121 --> 00:19:47,120
This is the device, if you would, that revolutionized naval warfare.

273
00:19:47,121 --> 00:19:54,120
During the course of the battle with the CSS Virginia, the monitor's turret was hit many times by her artillery,

274
00:19:54,121 --> 00:19:57,880
or artillery, including this potentially devastating hit,

275
00:19:57,881 --> 00:20:01,740
which distorted all eight layers of the armor

276
00:20:01,741 --> 00:20:04,800
and did not break through.

277
00:20:07,620 --> 00:20:09,820
Here are the Monitor's two gun ports.

278
00:20:09,821 --> 00:20:11,460
These were the openings in the turret

279
00:20:11,461 --> 00:20:13,980
for the guns to fire out of.

280
00:20:13,981 --> 00:20:16,540
And when the guns weren't firing out of them,

281
00:20:16,541 --> 00:20:19,640
they became weak spots in the turret's armor.

282
00:20:19,641 --> 00:20:23,520
So to make up for that, they had these large port stoppers.

283
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:26,720
As each of the monitor's cannons fired,

284
00:20:26,820 --> 00:20:29,860
it recoiled backwards and was loaded from the muzzle.

285
00:20:29,960 --> 00:20:31,920
This left an open port.

286
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:33,960
To protect the crew from enemy fire,

287
00:20:34,060 --> 00:20:36,960
a 1,200-pound wrought iron block was moved into place

288
00:20:37,060 --> 00:20:38,760
by a system of pulleys.

289
00:20:38,860 --> 00:20:41,800
Each time a gun fired, the port stoppers swung into place

290
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:43,780
to cover the open hole.

291
00:20:43,880 --> 00:20:46,480
During the battle, there were 21 men inside here,

292
00:20:46,580 --> 00:20:47,980
plus the two guns.

293
00:20:50,480 --> 00:20:51,620
It was dark.

294
00:20:51,720 --> 00:20:52,620
It was smoky.

295
00:20:52,621 --> 00:20:55,520
It must have been hellishly loud.

296
00:20:55,620 --> 00:21:01,100
The enemy fire clanging off the outside of the turret.

297
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:03,960
The guns themselves roaring and leaping.

298
00:21:07,400 --> 00:21:10,000
They were stretching the technology of the time

299
00:21:10,100 --> 00:21:14,940
to build this, and it proved to be quite successful.

300
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:20,140
The modern warship was born in the turret of the USS Monitor

301
00:21:20,141 --> 00:21:23,140
the USS Monitor in the year 1862.

302
00:21:30,980 --> 00:21:33,980
Whether on sea or land,

303
00:21:36,060 --> 00:21:38,820
transportation of troops and supplies

304
00:21:38,821 --> 00:21:41,820
is a centerpiece to any military battle plan.

305
00:21:44,340 --> 00:21:48,260
Civil War commanders realized this all too well

306
00:21:48,261 --> 00:21:57,400
and depended on new technology that accelerated transport strategy beyond any previous wartime conventions.

307
00:22:00,840 --> 00:22:07,180
The USS Monitor was the first naval vessel to have a below-the-water line flush toilet.

308
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:11,500
Civil War Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

309
00:22:11,501 --> 00:22:21,500
The modern military depends on a sophisticated transportation network to move troops and

310
00:22:21,501 --> 00:22:29,680
materiel to combat zones anywhere in the world.

311
00:22:29,681 --> 00:22:34,320
Civil war strategists made a monumental leap over their predecessors by employing a new

312
00:22:34,321 --> 00:22:38,720
mode of transportation, the Iron Horse.

313
00:22:38,721 --> 00:22:43,220
The railroads were relatively new innovation in American transportation.

314
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:48,880
And the military grabbed a hold of this technology to begin to utilize it,

315
00:22:48,881 --> 00:22:52,920
both strategically and to move both people, goods, munitions,

316
00:22:53,400 --> 00:22:56,340
very much like the modern military today.

317
00:22:59,740 --> 00:23:05,160
In 1860, a locomotive engine could travel at speeds of up to 35 miles per hour.

318
00:23:05,161 --> 00:23:10,880
A horse and wagon fully loaded could at best do 12 to 15 miles per hour.

319
00:23:11,780 --> 00:23:15,600
Those generals who kept railroads in mind generally succeeded.

320
00:23:15,960 --> 00:23:19,080
Any general who ignored a railroad would generally lose.

321
00:23:19,380 --> 00:23:24,900
In the autumn of 1863, an entire Union Corps, something like 20,000 plus men,

322
00:23:25,100 --> 00:23:27,900
were transported by train from Virginia to Tennessee.

323
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:31,140
That would have been out of the question in earlier times.

324
00:23:31,141 --> 00:23:39,420
the workhorse for the military on both sides was the american type 440 engine

325
00:23:39,421 --> 00:23:43,240
its streamlined power design was reliable and durable

326
00:23:43,241 --> 00:23:53,040
the fuel source was wood or coal burned in the engine's firebox the heat changed the water in

327
00:23:53,041 --> 00:23:58,440
the boiler to steam which was driven through pipes to the pistons the pistons generating a

328
00:23:58,441 --> 00:24:01,440
motion were connected to the main rod.

329
00:24:01,441 --> 00:24:05,440
This rod was attached to the wheels, creating a rotation,

330
00:24:05,441 --> 00:24:07,440
propelling the engine.

331
00:24:07,441 --> 00:24:10,440
And we'll look at the mechanics of running the

332
00:24:10,441 --> 00:24:15,440
locomotive, and the engineer, of course, had to be an expert at

333
00:24:15,441 --> 00:24:19,440
doing this because actually with this boiler, he was sitting on

334
00:24:19,441 --> 00:24:22,440
a bomb. If he didn't control the water level of boiler, he

335
00:24:22,441 --> 00:24:26,440
could have a boiler explosion. The actual mechanics to

336
00:24:26,441 --> 00:24:30,440
The actual mechanics to operate the engine were quite simple.

337
00:24:30,441 --> 00:24:34,440
The fireman loaded the wood or coal in the firebox.

338
00:24:34,441 --> 00:24:38,440
This is the Johnson bar for forward or reverse.

339
00:24:38,441 --> 00:24:42,440
The engine speed was regulated by this throttle lever.

340
00:24:42,441 --> 00:24:46,440
Pull back to accelerate or push forward to slow down.

341
00:24:46,441 --> 00:24:50,440
But the main focus for the engineer was the amount of water and steam

342
00:24:50,441 --> 00:24:54,440
regulated to the engine. The water level in the boiler had to be

343
00:24:54,441 --> 00:24:57,200
being maintained at the proper level.

344
00:24:57,201 --> 00:25:01,380
The engineer had three gauges to determine that level.

345
00:25:01,381 --> 00:25:06,260
He would always want the water to be above the first gauge,

346
00:25:06,261 --> 00:25:08,620
never to the third gauge.

347
00:25:08,621 --> 00:25:12,560
But the important thing is, if the water is too low,

348
00:25:12,561 --> 00:25:14,900
this could cause a boiler explosion.

349
00:25:14,901 --> 00:25:18,140
If he opened this valve here and got steam out of it

350
00:25:18,141 --> 00:25:22,360
rather than water, he's in a lot of trouble.

351
00:25:22,361 --> 00:25:26,740
In 1860, the North had about 21,000 miles of track,

352
00:25:26,741 --> 00:25:29,500
while the South had some 9,000 miles.

353
00:25:29,501 --> 00:25:31,660
However, without a system to manage all parts

354
00:25:31,661 --> 00:25:36,580
of the transportation network, nothing would go anywhere.

355
00:25:36,581 --> 00:25:39,540
In January 1862, the United States War

356
00:25:39,541 --> 00:25:42,760
Department created an agency called the US Military

357
00:25:42,761 --> 00:25:45,360
Railroad.

358
00:25:45,361 --> 00:25:48,340
Since at the time, the military had no experience

359
00:25:48,341 --> 00:25:50,680
in modern transportation, the Union

360
00:25:50,681 --> 00:25:52,720
employed civilian experts.

361
00:25:52,721 --> 00:25:55,560
Herman Haupt, a civil engineer who had constructed private

362
00:25:55,561 --> 00:25:58,740
railroads, was drafted to make the trains run on time.

363
00:25:58,741 --> 00:26:01,200
This is actually the first time in American history

364
00:26:01,201 --> 00:26:04,160
where the federal government took over, kind of,

365
00:26:04,161 --> 00:26:06,920
and formed its own private corporation to run a

366
00:26:06,921 --> 00:26:08,740
transportation system.

367
00:26:08,741 --> 00:26:12,000
Herman Haupt actually did this by purchasing locomotives,

368
00:26:12,001 --> 00:26:16,740
funding them, and creating his own railroad inside the federal

369
00:26:16,741 --> 00:26:17,740
government.

370
00:26:17,741 --> 00:26:21,280
His greatest achievement probably came in the Gettysburg campaign.

371
00:26:21,660 --> 00:26:24,080
He sent in crews. They built a new railroad.

372
00:26:24,380 --> 00:26:29,820
Herman Hauck was sending up about 1,500 tons of supplies per day to that army.

373
00:26:30,300 --> 00:26:34,040
Not only is that amazing, but the trains going back were not empty.

374
00:26:34,041 --> 00:26:37,360
He placed the wounded soldiers from Gettysburg on their trains,

375
00:26:37,800 --> 00:26:43,920
and some 15,000 of them were taken to military hospitals at rail heads such as Baltimore and Washington.

376
00:26:44,340 --> 00:26:46,720
It was a truly incredible accomplishment.

377
00:26:46,721 --> 00:26:49,780
There are about 7,000 books on Gettysburg.

378
00:26:50,080 --> 00:26:52,240
Very few ever mention Herman Haupt's name.

379
00:26:52,540 --> 00:26:55,120
He should be one of the leading heroes of that campaign.

380
00:26:58,500 --> 00:27:03,020
As the Civil War raged on and the North began moving into Southern Territory,

381
00:27:04,060 --> 00:27:07,020
Union trains encountered a huge technological obstacle

382
00:27:07,021 --> 00:27:10,220
that literally stopped them dead in their tracks.

383
00:27:11,020 --> 00:27:14,520
Railroad gauge in America is the distance between the two rails.

384
00:27:14,521 --> 00:27:17,400
In the north, standard gauge was, say, this wide.

385
00:27:17,640 --> 00:27:20,540
In the south, many of the railroads were, like, this wide,

386
00:27:20,680 --> 00:27:23,480
so the equipment couldn't fit on the tracks.

387
00:27:23,740 --> 00:27:24,460
It was that simple.

388
00:27:25,320 --> 00:27:28,060
The northern railroad track width, or gauge,

389
00:27:28,480 --> 00:27:30,780
was 4 feet 8 1⁄2 inches wide,

390
00:27:31,380 --> 00:27:33,960
derived from the British who pioneered railroad technology.

391
00:27:35,060 --> 00:27:37,200
However, the south used a variety of gauge.

392
00:27:37,660 --> 00:27:39,940
There was no standardized width for the tracks.

393
00:27:40,380 --> 00:27:43,960
Many of the armies literally had to pick up the tracks in the south

394
00:27:43,961 --> 00:27:46,520
and relay the track beds to standard gauge

395
00:27:46,521 --> 00:27:48,300
so that their locomotives could run on them.

396
00:27:48,840 --> 00:27:51,000
One of the great results of the American Civil War

397
00:27:51,001 --> 00:27:53,820
through the standardization of gauges north and south

398
00:27:53,821 --> 00:27:57,500
was the creation of a near-national network of railroading

399
00:27:57,501 --> 00:27:59,200
for the first time in American history.

400
00:28:06,380 --> 00:28:08,880
Once an army has been transported to the battlefield,

401
00:28:09,760 --> 00:28:11,560
communication and coordination

402
00:28:11,561 --> 00:28:14,320
are essential for every military operation.

403
00:28:20,020 --> 00:28:23,980
During the Civil War, orders sent by foot or horseback

404
00:28:23,981 --> 00:28:25,780
could take hours or days.

405
00:28:27,980 --> 00:28:30,780
A breakthrough technology gave commanders

406
00:28:30,781 --> 00:28:34,260
the unprecedented ability to communicate messages

407
00:28:34,261 --> 00:28:35,940
in mere seconds.

408
00:28:38,460 --> 00:28:41,480
The American railroad track width of four feet

409
00:28:41,481 --> 00:28:46,608
8 1⁄2 inches derives from the distance between the wheels on a Roman chariot.

410
00:28:46,609 --> 00:28:47,740
During the 1800s,

411
00:28:47,741 --> 00:28:52,260
the British used this standard for their railroad tracks, and America followed suit.

412
00:28:53,140 --> 00:28:56,060
Civil War Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

413
00:29:01,280 --> 00:29:08,000
In Iraq, today's military relies on an enormous telecommunication network to keep command and

414
00:29:08,001 --> 00:29:13,000
control in constant contact with troops at the front.

415
00:29:13,001 --> 00:29:21,800
At the same time, modern media can now transmit live broadcasts instantaneously from any remote

416
00:29:21,801 --> 00:29:22,800
war zone.

417
00:29:22,801 --> 00:29:30,340
But the first instant communication on the battlefield occurred during the American Civil

418
00:29:30,341 --> 00:29:31,340
War.

419
00:29:31,341 --> 00:29:36,360
One can make a good parallel between the telegraph and the railroad.

420
00:29:36,361 --> 00:29:41,060
shaped the course of the Civil War, both the foundations of modern-day transportation and

421
00:29:41,061 --> 00:29:41,974
communication.

422
00:29:41,975 --> 00:29:46,740
Samuel Morse, its inventor, sent the world's first telegraph message from

423
00:29:46,741 --> 00:29:51,469
Washington, D.C. to Baltimore, Maryland in 1844.

424
00:29:51,470 --> 00:29:56,100
The telegraph key is simply a switch that turns

425
00:29:56,101 --> 00:29:58,090
on or off electric current.

426
00:29:58,091 --> 00:30:02,880
As the sender taps out a message, the switch completes a circuit,

427
00:30:02,881 --> 00:30:06,040
which allows current to flow into the coil of the receiving machine.

428
00:30:07,820 --> 00:30:12,680
The iron bar above the coil is magnetized and attracts a second iron bar below it.

429
00:30:13,260 --> 00:30:18,520
When the key is released, shutting off the current, a spring forces the two bars apart.

430
00:30:19,600 --> 00:30:23,680
This attraction and deflection movement taps out the message on the receiving end.

431
00:30:24,740 --> 00:30:28,960
There's 50,000 miles of telegraph cable in this country,

432
00:30:28,961 --> 00:30:33,100
and in 1861, about the time of the start of the war,

433
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:36,880
there's a cable reaching from New York to San Francisco.

434
00:30:39,260 --> 00:30:44,780
In wars past, orders from commanders were sent by foot, horseback, or even carrier pigeon.

435
00:30:45,900 --> 00:30:49,180
Hours or even days passed before the information was received.

436
00:30:49,181 --> 00:30:53,740
The problem with the written dispatch, first it was on paper, second it could be lost,

437
00:30:53,980 --> 00:30:57,140
third it could be intercepted, and you really never know

438
00:30:57,141 --> 00:31:00,440
if that written dispatch ever made it to its destination.

439
00:31:03,140 --> 00:31:06,900
The telegraph reduced the time to send and receive a message to seconds

440
00:31:06,901 --> 00:31:10,820
and afforded generals an obvious advantage to outmaneuver the enemy.

441
00:31:11,360 --> 00:31:15,160
At 11 o'clock, if a general wants troops, he can push the telegraph key.

442
00:31:15,480 --> 00:31:19,540
At 1101, at some distant place, the troops are being alerted to get ready to move.

443
00:31:19,700 --> 00:31:21,120
It changes the face of war.

444
00:31:22,120 --> 00:31:26,160
When the Civil War began, telegraph lines were quickly erected everywhere

445
00:31:26,161 --> 00:31:27,493
the armies traveled.

446
00:31:27,494 --> 00:31:32,360
By the end of the war, more than 15,000 miles of telegraph lines had been

447
00:31:32,361 --> 00:31:35,784
built by both sides for military purposes only.

448
00:31:35,785 --> 00:31:39,280
The armies developed mobile telegraph units that

449
00:31:39,281 --> 00:31:42,297
could travel with the troops. General Ulysses S.

450
00:31:42,298 --> 00:31:45,440
Grant was a chief proponent of the new technology.

451
00:31:46,720 --> 00:31:50,021
What Grant did was basically get control of a continent.

452
00:31:50,022 --> 00:31:52,320
He had communications with a continent,

453
00:31:52,321 --> 00:31:57,100
and he is running a military operation that stretches for thousands of miles.

454
00:31:58,860 --> 00:32:01,940
The War Department opened its own telegraph office,

455
00:32:02,520 --> 00:32:07,580
and President Abraham Lincoln frequently spent hours receiving information about the war.

456
00:32:09,320 --> 00:32:12,980
It's probably one of the better examples of technology in warfare

457
00:32:12,981 --> 00:32:17,680
as to what was put down in 1861 and what we are using today.

458
00:32:17,681 --> 00:32:24,180
Use of the telegraph not only benefited the military,

459
00:32:24,680 --> 00:32:28,160
it also resulted in huge changes for the newspaper industry.

460
00:32:29,920 --> 00:32:33,940
The technology for the delivery of news during the Civil War

461
00:32:33,941 --> 00:32:36,620
was akin to the technology we have today

462
00:32:36,621 --> 00:32:39,780
in the sense that people had never experienced

463
00:32:39,781 --> 00:32:44,080
such rapid delivery of news about the war and its images.

464
00:32:44,081 --> 00:32:49,080
Civil War journalists were the first embedded reporters,

465
00:32:49,081 --> 00:32:52,080
covering the first instant news war.

466
00:32:52,081 --> 00:32:57,080
More than 500 correspondents covered the war from home front to front line,

467
00:32:57,081 --> 00:32:59,080
and the competition was fierce.

468
00:32:59,081 --> 00:33:04,080
The New York Herald during the Civil War spent something like $100,000 a year

469
00:33:04,081 --> 00:33:10,080
on war reporting between paying correspondence and paying for telegraph charges.

470
00:33:10,081 --> 00:33:14,820
Once news reports sped from the front via telegraph,

471
00:33:14,821 --> 00:33:17,620
a newly developed high-speed printing press,

472
00:33:18,020 --> 00:33:20,760
perfected by Philadelphia inventor William Bullock,

473
00:33:21,540 --> 00:33:24,040
churned out newspapers at an astonishing rate.

474
00:33:28,740 --> 00:33:32,660
Prior to Bullock's press, only 1,500 news copies an hour

475
00:33:32,661 --> 00:33:34,200
could be printed by a hand press.

476
00:33:36,960 --> 00:33:39,500
Bullock's patented steam-powered press

477
00:33:39,501 --> 00:33:44,620
which could produce about 10,000 pages an hour

478
00:33:44,621 --> 00:33:49,040
and operated by feeding off a continuous roll of newsprint

479
00:33:49,041 --> 00:33:55,280
rather than requiring individual sheets of newsprint being fed into the press

480
00:33:55,281 --> 00:34:01,780
and had the advantage over the older presses of printing both sides of the newspaper at once.

481
00:34:02,820 --> 00:34:07,600
The telegraph and high-speed presses signaled a giant leap forward for mass media.

482
00:34:07,601 --> 00:34:12,200
For the first time, it was possible to read what happened on a distant battlefield just

483
00:34:12,201 --> 00:34:13,360
days after it occurred.

484
00:34:14,760 --> 00:34:19,300
Crowds would wait outside the big dailies in New York, waiting for the news.

485
00:34:19,660 --> 00:34:22,360
The newspapers got used to publishing extra editions.

486
00:34:23,680 --> 00:34:28,080
It was also during the Civil War that both the glory and the horror of war were first

487
00:34:28,081 --> 00:34:29,060
captured on camera.

488
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:34,660
Matthew Brady and his associates recorded the images of war and brought them to the American

489
00:34:34,661 --> 00:34:35,361
public.

490
00:34:37,600 --> 00:34:43,480
Employing what is called a wet plate process,

491
00:34:44,120 --> 00:34:46,600
photographers used chemically treated glass plates

492
00:34:46,601 --> 00:34:49,320
that could actually be developed on the battlefield.

493
00:34:51,000 --> 00:34:55,740
This technological marvel captured realistic images

494
00:34:55,741 --> 00:34:58,440
showing the devastation of our soldiers,

495
00:34:58,800 --> 00:35:00,100
the agony on their faces,

496
00:35:00,101 --> 00:35:05,560
and the absolute horrors of war firsthand in studio fashion.

497
00:35:07,600 --> 00:35:20,480
Technology may have fueled the misery of war.

498
00:35:20,940 --> 00:35:24,420
However, innovations in battlefield medicine and field rations

499
00:35:24,421 --> 00:35:27,140
would make a soldier's life somewhat more bearable.

500
00:35:28,840 --> 00:35:34,920
In 1867, William Bullock, inventor of the high-speed steam Bullock printing press,

501
00:35:34,921 --> 00:35:39,880
met an untimely death when he became entrapped in the gears of his invention.

502
00:35:41,040 --> 00:35:43,760
Civil War Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

503
00:35:48,840 --> 00:35:54,540
Today's American armed forces benefit from some of the best food and medical care in military history.

504
00:35:57,000 --> 00:36:00,900
The concepts of providing soldiers with portable, nutritious meals

505
00:36:00,901 --> 00:36:04,020
and employing life-saving combat medical procedures

506
00:36:04,021 --> 00:36:06,900
took giant steps forward during the Civil War.

507
00:36:07,260 --> 00:36:09,660
Many of the technological advances,

508
00:36:10,040 --> 00:36:12,660
advances in surgery, sanitation, dietetics,

509
00:36:13,440 --> 00:36:16,900
can all be traced directly back from our modern military today,

510
00:36:17,080 --> 00:36:18,680
right back to the American Civil War.

511
00:36:21,440 --> 00:36:23,860
Getting food to the troops during any conflict

512
00:36:23,861 --> 00:36:25,240
is always top priority.

513
00:36:26,660 --> 00:36:29,200
However, during the initial phases of the Civil War,

514
00:36:29,201 --> 00:36:33,180
soldiers existed on a biscuit wafer made of water and flour

515
00:36:33,181 --> 00:36:38,120
that was almost impossible to chew, appropriately known as hardtack.

516
00:36:39,340 --> 00:36:41,180
Occasionally they would get salted beef,

517
00:36:41,720 --> 00:36:45,720
but you had to be careful of the beef because more often than not it's going to be rancid.

518
00:36:46,160 --> 00:36:48,500
But hardtack and coffee were the basic diets,

519
00:36:48,800 --> 00:36:54,260
and this is why digestive systems among these soldiers are going to be seriously impaired.

520
00:36:54,261 --> 00:37:02,840
Civil War brass employed a new technology that would supply the troops with healthy, unspoiled food anywhere on the front lines.

521
00:37:04,700 --> 00:37:05,400
Canned food.

522
00:37:08,040 --> 00:37:14,080
Annual production of canned foods increased from 5 million to 30 million by the end of the Civil War.

523
00:37:15,700 --> 00:37:19,060
Surprisingly, some Civil War canned products have endured to today.

524
00:37:20,920 --> 00:37:22,960
Such as Underwood deviled ham.

525
00:37:22,961 --> 00:37:30,840
Leigh and Perrin's Worcestershire sauce, Borden's condensed milk, and Van Camp's pork and beans.

526
00:37:32,860 --> 00:37:41,180
The canning process dates back to 1795 in France, when Emperor Napoleon Bonaparte was concerned about keeping his armies fed.

527
00:37:44,180 --> 00:37:48,480
Nicolas Appare conceived the idea of preserving food in bottles, like wine.

528
00:37:48,481 --> 00:37:54,520
He realized if food is sufficiently heated and sealed in an airtight container, it won't spoil.

529
00:37:57,020 --> 00:38:01,000
By the 1800s, bottles were replaced by tin cans.

530
00:38:01,720 --> 00:38:09,500
And in 1858, food cans became mass-produced when a Baltimore oyster canner dramatically sped up the cooking process.

531
00:38:10,900 --> 00:38:16,940
Isaac Solomon discovered that by adding salt to water, it increased the boiling point of water,

532
00:38:16,941 --> 00:38:19,160
and therefore they could cook the food more quickly.

533
00:38:20,380 --> 00:38:24,960
The process of combining salt with water requires more heat to bring water to a boil.

534
00:38:25,880 --> 00:38:29,960
Since the water temperature is hotter, the food inside the cans cooks faster,

535
00:38:30,540 --> 00:38:34,780
reducing the preparation time from five or six hours to 30 minutes.

536
00:38:34,781 --> 00:38:39,240
They increased from 2,000 cans a day to 20,000 cans a day,

537
00:38:39,620 --> 00:38:45,720
therefore increasing the process and making the food safer for shipment in the cans across the country.

538
00:38:46,940 --> 00:38:51,940
The Union Army took advantage of this canning technology immediately.

539
00:38:51,941 --> 00:38:56,940
Soon, fruits, vegetables, and meats found their way to the camps in cans.

540
00:38:56,941 --> 00:39:03,940
The rations the men are using today go right straight back to the innovation of canned goods in the 1860s.

541
00:39:03,941 --> 00:39:09,940
With better prepared and preserved rations, troops certainly ate healthier.

542
00:39:09,941 --> 00:39:15,940
However, life on the front line was still a deadly business.

543
00:39:15,941 --> 00:39:22,560
During the Civil War, an estimated 250,000 soldiers died on the battlefield.

544
00:39:24,900 --> 00:39:32,740
At the start of the war, fallen men were cared for by a woefully underqualified, understaffed, and undersupplied medical corps.

545
00:39:33,560 --> 00:39:39,100
In many circumstances, a wounded soldier would languish on the battlefield for days before being retrieved.

546
00:39:39,101 --> 00:39:42,700
The military hierarchy, the top brass, if you will,

547
00:39:42,701 --> 00:39:48,480
was still very much of the feeling that the medicine should wait until the battle is over.

548
00:39:49,920 --> 00:39:53,200
Dr. Jonathan Letterman, medical director of the Union Army,

549
00:39:53,340 --> 00:39:55,760
was considered the father of battlefield medicine.

550
00:39:57,840 --> 00:40:00,940
Letterman developed a rescue system for evacuating the wounded

551
00:40:00,941 --> 00:40:06,720
and established ambulances operated by dedicated military personnel assigned to each regiment.

552
00:40:06,721 --> 00:40:12,460
A wounded soldier could now be taken off the battlefield and admitted into a field hospital within hours.

553
00:40:13,560 --> 00:40:19,080
This is a typical ambulance wagon, outfitted with water casks and empty stretchers,

554
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:23,000
used to evacuate wounded soldiers from the battlefield carnage.

555
00:40:23,480 --> 00:40:27,620
Typically, these wounded were stacked laterally left and right to Hive

556
00:40:27,621 --> 00:40:31,260
as they were evacuated from the battlefield to the rearward hospitals.

557
00:40:32,600 --> 00:40:36,260
Anesthesia, such as chloroform, was introduced in the 1840s.

558
00:40:36,720 --> 00:40:39,360
but had its first widespread use during the war.

559
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:44,620
For more efficient delivery, new devices were developed for its use.

560
00:40:45,500 --> 00:40:47,200
This is called the Chisholm inhaler.

561
00:40:47,260 --> 00:40:48,800
It was developed by the Confederacy.

562
00:40:48,801 --> 00:40:51,040
By having a small sponge inside,

563
00:40:51,940 --> 00:40:57,420
we were able to concentrate 100% of the ether or chloroform into the patient.

564
00:40:57,840 --> 00:40:59,660
A few drops were to be put on the screen.

565
00:41:00,060 --> 00:41:02,200
It would then be inserted into the nose of the patient,

566
00:41:02,480 --> 00:41:04,980
and a very small quantity of chloroform or ether

567
00:41:04,981 --> 00:41:06,960
could then render the patient unconscious.

568
00:41:09,420 --> 00:41:12,580
Civil War surgeons not only saved lives on the battlefield,

569
00:41:13,340 --> 00:41:16,360
but with new technology, they were the first to attempt cosmetic

570
00:41:16,361 --> 00:41:18,980
and reconstructive surgery after a patient recovered.

571
00:41:19,900 --> 00:41:23,440
Men who had very traumatic wounds, especially to the face,

572
00:41:23,460 --> 00:41:27,140
that affected their appearance, could actually find relief

573
00:41:27,141 --> 00:41:31,180
from a surgeon who could reconstruct that part of the face

574
00:41:31,181 --> 00:41:32,540
that was very badly damaged.

575
00:41:32,541 --> 00:41:35,540
We have one excellent example of a soldier.

576
00:41:35,541 --> 00:41:42,540
The wound included parts of his nose, his cheekbone, and his upper lip, very badly damaged.

577
00:41:42,541 --> 00:41:47,540
The cosmetic surgery closed the hole in the wound, but of course left severe scarring.

578
00:41:47,541 --> 00:41:50,540
A prosthetic device was then developed.

579
00:41:50,541 --> 00:41:58,540
A nose piece to cover the nose, a large mustache to cover the missing part of the upper lip, was hung on a pair of glasses.

580
00:41:58,541 --> 00:42:03,140
The glasses could be placed on, the nose then would be in the correct place,

581
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:05,040
and he could lead a very normal life,

582
00:42:05,200 --> 00:42:08,280
appearing to be very normal to anyone who saw him on the street.

583
00:42:10,980 --> 00:42:15,200
Amputation was a common surgical technique performed on patients with severe wounds.

584
00:42:15,860 --> 00:42:18,640
However, new prosthetics came of age during the Civil War

585
00:42:18,641 --> 00:42:21,340
that gave patients maximum mobility after surgery.

586
00:42:22,320 --> 00:42:26,240
We have a leg here, just a peg leg like you would expect to see on a pirate,

587
00:42:26,241 --> 00:42:29,740
very heavy, made of heavy leather, wood, and metal.

588
00:42:29,741 --> 00:42:32,940
Not very functional, not very good looking,

589
00:42:32,941 --> 00:42:35,040
and not very comfortable for the wearer.

590
00:42:35,041 --> 00:42:38,440
On the other hand, we have a very technologically advanced arm

591
00:42:38,441 --> 00:42:39,940
from 1863.

592
00:42:39,941 --> 00:42:43,540
It has vent holes to allow for free air circulation.

593
00:42:43,541 --> 00:42:46,540
We have an articulated elbow for natural movement.

594
00:42:46,541 --> 00:42:48,740
We have an opposable thumb on a natural hand.

595
00:42:48,741 --> 00:42:51,440
That thumb can actually allow the wearer to grasp.

596
00:42:51,441 --> 00:42:54,440
And over the whole arm, we have a paint color

597
00:42:54,441 --> 00:42:58,020
that is going to more closely approximate human skin color.

598
00:42:58,380 --> 00:43:02,220
This is the technological beginnings of modern prosthesis.

599
00:43:02,820 --> 00:43:06,620
It started a trend of fit and function

600
00:43:06,621 --> 00:43:10,520
that today is seen in the modern sea leg.

601
00:43:11,320 --> 00:43:15,420
The sea leg is the most modern leg

602
00:43:15,421 --> 00:43:17,360
used by the United States military.

603
00:43:17,361 --> 00:43:24,360
In today's battlefield medicine,

604
00:43:24,361 --> 00:43:27,360
along with advancements in weapons,

605
00:43:27,361 --> 00:43:30,360
warships,

606
00:43:30,361 --> 00:43:34,360
transportation,

607
00:43:34,361 --> 00:43:38,360
and communication,

608
00:43:38,361 --> 00:43:42,360
can all trace their technological origins

609
00:43:42,361 --> 00:43:46,360
back to the war that altered the course of American history.

610
00:43:46,361 --> 00:43:47,360
history.

611
00:43:51,260 --> 00:43:54,800
And now this technology helps the American military

612
00:43:54,900 --> 00:43:57,600
in the 21st century.

613
00:43:57,700 --> 00:44:00,540
The technologies we have today in war,

614
00:44:00,640 --> 00:44:03,580
as well as the technologies we're going to have tomorrow

615
00:44:03,680 --> 00:44:08,220
in war, all go back to the Civil War itself, where,

616
00:44:08,320 --> 00:44:11,780
for the first time in history, men began to use technology.

617
00:44:11,880 --> 00:44:13,420
And they used it with a capital T,

618
00:44:13,421 --> 00:44:16,420
knowing that this is the way wars have to be fought.

619
00:44:16,421 --> 00:44:17,420
Fire!

